Date: Sun, 14 Nov 93 15:07:29 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: MOVIE: "Fearless" is a good movie, IMO

"Fearless" is a thought-provoking movie, starring Jeff Bridges and
directed by Peter Weir.

Briefly, Bridges survives a plane crash virtually unscathed and then
acts as if he is a kind of ghost, unable to be injured or killed. He
encounters other survivors, including a woman (Rosie Perez, who drops
her ditzy image and does a fine acting job) who lost her baby in the
crash.

Like many excellent movies, it exposed me to a world I've never seen
(and hope never to in person), namely, a plane crash. This was not
just a quick glimpse, but a faitful reconstruction. (And yet it was
not especially morbid.)

As Bridges goes about his life in San Francisco, we see him acting
like the walking dead. How, or if, he adjusts is the theme.

The ObExtropian content is the concern with the nature of life and
death and the reactions people have. 

So far, this is the best "serious" movie I've seen this year. (Not
counting things like "Jurassic Park," "Demolition Man," and any of the
fine thrillers this past summer.)

A great movie to provoke conversation over.

--Tim May

(Sorry for not tying it in to the various flame wars over ExI,
unsubscribes, James Donald, defamation, and banality now raging on the
list...whoops, I just _did_. Never mind.)

