Date: Sun, 14 Nov 93 16:43:31 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Contentiousness and Collegiality

A brief comment on why I think we are so contentious on this list.

Let me cite an example, a seemingly trivial example. I wrote:

> So far, this is the best "serious" movie I've seen this year. (Not
> counting things like "Jurassic Park," "Demolition Man," and any of the
> fine thrillers this past summer.)

Now it hasn't happened yet, and it may not happen at all, but imagine
someone who really liked "Jurassic Park" taking issue with my
(apparent) dismissal of "JP" as a "serious" movie.

(Sorry to pick a completely hypothetical situation, but I thought it
would make the point without needlessly angering others.)

Too often we zero in on what we disagree with, as as Kennita noted.
But I disagree with her that it mainly has to do with the personality
types we are, our desire to prove our intelligence, etc.

No, I think the problem in all these mailing lists and newsgroups is
that the audience is just too large. Not large by world-changing
standards, but incredibly large if viewed as a conversational group.
With hundreds of readers--though perhaps only a few dozen who actually
read a post in detail, for whatever reasons--someone is nearly certain
to be bugged about _something_.

A situation related to how classrooms and lectures get bogged down in
debate from the audience. The reason why questions are often saved til
the end.

Hal Finney urged us to move away from the "lecture" mode, away from
the mode in which we each give little speeches (like here). Well, I
fear Hal's goals are mostly unachievable. And maybe undesirable, given
the tendencies noted here.

The most useful and enjoyable threads for me have been things like the
AIT Virtual Seminar, the "Predicting the Stock Market" thread, and the
recent (a week and a half ago) debate started by Nick Szabo on "New
Science." In both cases, a mix of essays--to get the ball rolling--and
a lot of back-and-forth debate.

(I think I've seen similar situtions in other threads, including
several I was not prepared to participate in, so it's not just the
threads I personally liked I'm referring to.)

In these wonderful threads, the "hit and run" posters were largely
left behind, as an emergent phenomenon, and a fairly collegial
atmosphere evolved (even if Nick was dead wrong about physics still
being a fresh field! :-}).

This collegiality is what I seek. Not emotional support, not to prove
my intelligence, not to win arguments.

Just interesting discussion.

Those who feel the same should try to initiate new threads on
interesting topics, as Nick did a while back and as many others have
done as well (I don't mean to slight anyone, this is just fresh in my
mind).

Personally, I'd like to see the "randomness and the market" thread
resumed, and will soon post some further thoughts on this.

--Tim May




