Subject: Kangaroos (and defamation)

At a slight tangent to this thread ...  . Under current law, publishing a
statement of the form "A says that B murdered his wife, but I do not
believe it" is actionable as libel. I happen to know that because I
discussed it with a colleague recently in a different context. According to
what he told me, if Perot says on nationwide TV that Gore is an
axe-murderer and the New York Times reports it, the Times is guilty of
libel--although, of course, it is not likely that Gore would sue. They are
not saved by Times v Sullivan, the case which restricted libel in cases
involving a public figure. Under the Sullivan standard, it is libel to say
something libelous about even a public figure if you believe it is
false--and the Times, in my hypothetical, obviously would believe it was
false that Gore is an axe-murderer. Attributing the libel (truthfully) to
someone else is no defense--you have still published it.

