> security? Can we switch to the out-of-band SMTP routing info, which is MUCH
> more secure (although not completely, dammit)?

  Not really. Telnet to panix smtp, MAIL FROM, RCPT TO, DATA, and you're done
or sendmail -f if you are the owner of your mail machine. Using "From "
would be easy but it wouldn't provide any additional security except through
obscurity, and it would cause problems for people for whom "From " =~ "From: "
The only way to really block forgery is with authentication. We don't
need to go to full PGP for the kind of security we need since our channel
is fairly secure against James Donald eavesdropping. A simple 
password/signature code system would work well and wouldn't need special
software on the user's side. Just require each user to attach a small
password/signature to his message (it could be put into his .sig file)
which is filtered out by the software before it is posted. If the signature
isn't present, the message isn't posted. Unless you gave out your password,
or James Donald snooped your e-mail there is no need to worry.

  The level of crypto used should relate to the value of the resource you
are trying to protect. You don't always have to go to full 1024-bit RSA
encryption with IDEA for every job. PGP would be overkill for the list
software and would cost a lot more CPU time.


